We establish the optimal Lipschitz regularity of very weak solutions for the measure-valued elliptic PDE $-\mathrm{div}(A(x) \nabla u) = Q \; \mathcal{H}^{n-1} \llcorner \Gamma$ within a smooth domain $\Omega$ of $\mathbb{R}^n$. Here, $\Gamma$ represents a $C^{1,\alpha}$-smooth hypersurface, with $Q$ defined as a $C^{0,\alpha}$ density on $\Gamma$, and $A$ characterizes a symmetric, uniformly elliptic and $W^{1,q}$-regular tensor $(q > n)$. Furthermore, the article examines the optimality of these conditions applied to the problem data. This particular equation essentially represents a special interface coupling of two $A$-harmonic functions, with implications for free boundary problems, to be discussed in detail. Subsequently, the article extends these findings to a broader class of nonlinear PDEs, such as those presented in the context of the Hungerford--Vázquez degenerate parabolic equation at the interface of a puncture. In this degenerate scenario, the article proves the local $W^{1,\infty}$-regularity of very weak solutions under the assumption that the initial data possesses a limited value, considered finite. Importantly, these findings address a previously unresolved issue in the field as raised in \cite{Munkres1963} and have been investigated extensively in the context of the buzzworthy "5/6" theorem, specifically for the case where $n = 6$ and $\Gamma$ exhibits $C^{1,\alpha}$ regularity, with $\alpha > 0$; it was discovered that this particular case proves to be more challenging and requires additional constraints - the $\Gamma$ must also be compact and Riemannian, with a constant intrinsic curvature of $\mathcal{F}^0$ - Curvature of $\mathcal{F}$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$.

