The development of models for stellar, substellar, and planetary atmospheres heavily relies on accurate input data, specifically opacities, but various assumptions in existing models often lead to inconsistencies. In this context, we have compiled a comprehensive database of pre-calculated synthetic spectra for M-type stars based on updated atomic and molecular data, covering a wide wavelength range of 0.36-40 ${m \mu m}$ and spanning a diverse grid of 112 model stellar parameters. These models have been validated through comparisons with actual star spectra, and our analysis also highlights the impact of line list modifications on synthetic spectra, underlining the significance of incorporating updated atomic and molecular data in atmosphere models to ensure reliable predictions. Furthermore, we have observed that including standard atomic data in models for hot Jovian planets yields satisfactory results but only through the use of very low surface gravity values, even with updated molecular data. In contrast, including line lists with low completeness in data for M-type planets, whether they are substellar or planetary, often leads to significant errors in spectral classification. Additionally, we have determined the essential spectral features of M-type planets and their implications for the accurate identification of hot Jovian planets. Finally, our database of synthetic spectra is available online for further use in atmospheric model applications. { f This research has been enabled by the computational resources provided to the PI M. G. P. Bosu on Einstein Toolbox's DC cluster.} \vspace{1.0mm} 
